-- ============================================================================================
-- 🔴 MATCHING AP'S NAME TO OURS IS WHERE THIS GOES WRONG, SO EVERY ROW IS PINNED BY ID
-- ============================================================================================
-- At 435 races a per-race eyeball is not a method. What was done instead:
--
--   1. Match generated in code: AP's winner surname against our candidates WITHIN the race,
--      keyed on `districts.geo_id` = state FIPS + AP's own `censusDistrict` (which is "00" for
--      the six at-large states, where AP's `seatNum` says "1"). Accents are folded in the
--      generator, because `unaccent` IS NOT INSTALLED ON PRODUCTION.
--   2. 378 of 378 matched, every one unique within its race, zero ambiguities.
--   3. 🔴 THAT UNIFORM ANSWER WAS TREATED AS A BROKEN DETECTOR UNTIL TWO PLANTED FAULTS TURNED
--      IT RED: an unmatchable surname on AL-01 (matched fell to 377, 1 problem) and a cloned
--      same-name candidate in AL-02 (1 ambiguity). Each tamper was asserted present in the file
--      before the run, because a tamper that did not apply is a control that proved nothing.
--   4. Only the 88 rows where AP's spelling and ours DIFFER were read by hand. The other
--      289 agree on first and last name after folding, so there is nothing to judge.
--   5. Party cross-check: AP's party against ours on all 302 rows where ours is non-null - zero
--      clashes, and a planted party flip proved that check can fail.
--
-- 🔴 THE MATCH IS NOT RE-DERIVED HERE. Each row below is pinned by `race_candidates.id`, and
-- the gate re-asserts that the pinned row really is in that district and really carries that
-- surname. A mistyped id raises instead of silently moving a win to another seat.
--
